From: mtls-hello project
Date: 2026-08-06
Subject: [PATCH] Document why SSL_CLIENT_CERT is not in ssl_hook_Fixup_vars

This patch is intentionally a no-op at runtime. It only adds a comment
upstream of Apache httpd's mod_ssl to explain why `SSL_CLIENT_CERT` is not
included in the `ssl_hook_Fixup_vars` list and how to obtain the full PEM
certificate for unverified, self-signed client certificates.

For this project, the practical workaround is to use Apache's
`SSLOptions +ExportCertData` directive together with `SSLVerifyClient
optional_no_ca`. That combination exports `SSL_CLIENT_CERT` to CGI even when
the verification result is `FAILED:self-signed certificate`, so the handler
can fingerprint, capture, or reject the certificate at the application layer.

If this project ever needs to fork mod_ssl again, this patch documents the
exact location where the export behavior could be changed. Applying it is not
required for the current implementation.

--- a/modules/ssl/ssl_engine_kernel.c
+++ b/modules/ssl/ssl_engine_kernel.c
@@ -1501,6 +1501,15 @@ static const char *const ssl_hook_Fixup_vars[] = {
     "SSL_CLIENT_VERIFY",
     "SSL_CLIENT_M_VERSION",
     "SSL_CLIENT_M_SERIAL",
+    /*
+     * NOTE: SSL_CLIENT_CERT is intentionally absent from this list.
+     * It is exported only under SSL_OPT_EXPORTCERTDATA, i.e. when the
+     * operator uses "SSLOptions +ExportCertData". With "SSLVerifyClient
+     * optional_no_ca" that still exposes the full PEM certificate even for
+     * self-signed/unverified client certificates, which is enough for
+     * application-layer trust evaluation and purgatory capture.
+     * Adding it here would expose it with +StdEnvVars as well, but is not
+     * necessary for the current project.
+     */
     "SSL_CLIENT_V_START",
     "SSL_CLIENT_V_END",
     "SSL_CLIENT_V_REMAIN",
